capture: container and application startup
runtime: JDK 8
commit: c8f6226105
source: $TOMCAT_HOME/logs/catalina.out
produced-by: docs/migration/smoke-evidence/smoke-checks.sh
region-status: SKIPPED
skip-reason: no six-service reference stack and no servlet container on this host,
  therefore no container log to mine.  Measured, not assumed: the hostname
  arkcase-ce.local does not resolve, and all five documented service URLs answered
  000 with no HTTP response at all; there is no Tomcat 9 installation and
  TOMCAT_HOME is unset; no arkcase.war exists anywhere in the working tree; and
  consequently $TOMCAT_HOME/logs/catalina.out does not exist, with CATALINA_LOG
  unset.  This region cannot be captured without a live deployment.  The older
  runtime is deliberately NOT listed among the unmet preconditions, because it is
  not one: a JDK 8 installation is present here and answers 1.8.0_492, and the
  sibling env/toolchain.txt records that same banner for the baseline side.  What
  is missing is the deployment and its backing services, not the JVM.
normalised: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  The policy is stated in full
  anyway, so that the mirror reproduces the transformation rather than guesses at
  it.  When this region is captured, the pipeline strips timestamps and dates,
  thread names and thread identifiers, session identifiers and generated request
  and instance identifiers, absolute filesystem paths in favour of a stable
  token, port assignments, and elapsed-time and duration figures.  It preserves
  the behaviour-bearing content: component, bean and class names, counts of any
  kind, lifecycle and state transitions, the archive and context name, and the
  full message text of every warning and error line.  Over-normalising this
  region would destroy the very evidence it exists to carry.
redacted: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  When it is captured, every
  credential-shaped value is replaced with the fixed placeholder <REDACTED>,
  which is the convention this documentation tree already uses.  Three carriers
  are in scope: the documented administrator credential, which is never
  transcribed here in any form; keystore and trust-store passwords, which a
  container that echoes its launch arguments would emit, and which are recorded
  only in the redacted forms keyStorePassword=<REDACTED> and
  trustStorePassword=<REDACTED>; and any datasource or message-broker credential
  echoed at startup, which is treated identically.
