notes:
- The capture delimiters above are deliberately empty.  Every capture in this
  directory is script-produced; a hand-authored capture is not evidence.  Because
  the preconditions recorded in skip-reason were not available, no log line was
  invented to fill the gap.  A fabricated baseline would make every
  behaviour-preserved claim in this migration unfalsifiable, which defeats the
  baseline-as-tie-breaker rule outright.  An explicit, machine-readable skip with
  a reason is rule-compliant; an invented capture is not, and is strictly worse
  than an honest absence.
- A SKIPPED region is NOT a pass, and two matching SKIPPED regions across
  baseline and migrated are NOT evidence of preserved behaviour.  They record an
  absence in both places.  The sibling notes/flows-not-captured.txt reaches the
  same verdict for the eight application flows and records the comparator's
  overall INCONCLUSIVE-STACK-NEVER-READY result; this region is consistent with
  that record rather than an exception to it.
- produced-by names the designated producer of this region, not a claim that the
  producer emitted content on this run.  It emitted none, which is why the
  delimiters are empty.  That script refuses to start without an administrator
  credential supplied through its environment, so no run was possible here even
  setting the absent stack aside, and supplying administrator material would have
  been neither available nor appropriate.
- Region-selection rule, stated here so the migrated capture reproduces it
  without guesswork.  Archive the behaviour-bearing region of the first-startup
  window, not an unbounded raw dump: the deployment of the archive; container
  identity and connector setup; context start through the point the application
  answers at its own context path; and every startup warning and error line
  exactly as observed.  The window opens when the archive is placed in the
  container's deployment directory and closes when the application answers, and
  the whole window is in scope.
- Truncating the window early is the single easiest way to produce a region that
  looks clean because the interesting part had not happened yet.  The first
  startup is slow by design and the capture must span all of it.
- Territory boundaries, so the mirror diff stays mechanical and nothing is
  double-counted.  This region covers container and application startup only.
  Toolchain provenance belongs to env/toolchain.txt, artifact digests to
  artifacts/, archived unit-test reports to surefire/, and the eight application
  flows to the flow captures.  Spring context initialisation, process-engine
  deployment, messaging and persistence initialisation, the startup asset build
  and the error-severity scan are separate sibling region files drawn from this
  same window.  None of them is duplicated here.
- The archive directory is startup/, and that name is load-bearing rather than
  cosmetic.  The repository's root ignore file uses bare, leading-slash-free
  patterns, and those match at any depth: logs, bin, target, activemq-data and
  partition-descriptor are all ignored wherever they appear, as is builds/.  The
  ignored token is always the DIRECTORY name and never the .log file extension,
  so a .log file placed inside a directory named logs would be silently
  uncommitted while every local check still passed.  This file therefore sits
  directly in startup/ and no subdirectory was created beneath it.
- The pointed irony is worth recording: the SOURCE of this capture is
  $TOMCAT_HOME/logs/catalina.out, which lives in a logs directory, while the
  ARCHIVE directory is startup/ precisely because logs/ cannot be committed here.
  Reading FROM such a directory is fine; writing INTO one is the trap.
- Governing rule on discovered defects, verbatim: "Pre-existing bugs discovered
  during the work are documented rather than fixed, unless one blocks a
  validation item."  Applied to this region that means every startup warning is
  captured exactly as observed, with nothing repaired, nothing retried into
  submission and nothing hidden.  The migration's escape clause is invoked
  exactly twice in the entire change set and neither invocation is in this
  folder.  Nothing in this file proposes, implies or performs a fix.
- Governing rule on evidence, verbatim: "Validation asserts on produced artifacts
  and captured output, never on process exit status alone. This is mandatory
  rather than stylistic because Gruntfile.js sets grunt.option('force', true),
  which masks task failures and lets a broken build exit zero."  This bites
  directly here.  The startup-time asset build runs through that same
  forced-execution pipeline, so a deployment can reach ready state with a broken
  asset build.  The captured log region is the evidence; the deployment's exit
  status is not, and a healthy readiness probe is not either.
- Governing rule on ambiguity, verbatim: "The application's observed behavior at
  the base commit on JDK 8 is the tie-breaker for any ambiguity, and each
  resolution must be documented."
  [Editorial note: the rule's own text names the older runtime with a phrase this
  documentation tree's wording gate forbids; the substitution changes no meaning
  and is disclosed here rather than made silently.]
  That rule is the whole reason this file exists before any migrated counterpart
  does, and the reason an absence is recorded instead of a plausible-looking
  substitute: an unrun startup's behaviour is unknowable rather than assumable.
- Provenance of the rules quoted above, and both facts belong together because
  either alone misleads.  First, the project's rules facility reports, verbatim,
  "No user rules provided" - there is no on-disk user rules document for this
  project, and that is explicitly not licence to lower the bar.  Second, rules
  are nonetheless present and binding: the requirements embed an explicit,
  numbered block of seven rules that governs this work in full exactly as an
  external rules document would, alongside seven transformation rules.  The
  identifiers the migration plan uses for them are its own navigational
  convention rather than quoted titles, which is why the rules are quoted here by
  their text rather than cited by number alone.
- The launch configuration audited for the rule against relying on JDK-internal
  access is, at commit c8f6226105, the block at README.md:111-127, duplicated at
  docs/setup.md:76-96.  It is described here and cited by anchor, and
  deliberately never reproduced verbatim, for two reasons: it carries
  credential-shaped values, and it also carries two pre-existing unbalanced
  quotation marks, at README.md:119 and spanning README.md:123-124.  Importing
  that block would copy a pre-existing defect into a new file for no benefit,
  and the rule on discovered defects forbids repairing it, so it is neither
  repaired nor imported.
- Verified at the base commit and after migration: that launch configuration
  contains zero module-access JVM arguments.  If such an argument ever appeared
  in a captured banner it would be replaced, in this region, by the description
  "a module-access JVM argument that opens or exports an otherwise-encapsulated
  JDK package to the unnamed module", and the substitution would be recorded in
  this notes block.  The register of applied exceptions is referred to here by
  title only, as the JDK access exceptions register, because its filename embeds
  a token this tree excludes; it is delivered empty with a positive statement
  that none are required.
- Credential posture, in the repository's own voice rather than a policy invented
  here.  At the base commit docs/setup.md:78 states that real keystore and
  trust-store passwords must not be checked into source control and should
  instead be supplied through the environment or a managed credential store in
  any non-local environment, and that page already renders both values as
  <REDACTED> at :86 and :87 and the administrator password as <REDACTED> at :119,
  where README.md does not.  This capture honours that posture.  The documented
  administrator password appears at exactly one location tree-wide, in README.md,
  and it is not transcribed anywhere in this directory - not even as an
  illustration of what redaction removes.
- Registered observation, not a defect report against this file and not something
  to fix: the current working tree has moved past the base commit, so the anchors
  cited above are cited as base-commit anchors and should be re-read at commit
  c8f6226105.  The migration reflowed the duplicated launch-configuration block
  in docs/setup.md into a verbatim copy of the README's, which that page states
  as its explicit intent, so the line numbers of the surrounding prose differ in
  the current tree.  Anchors are given at the revision this baseline describes,
  which is the only revision at which a baseline is meaningful.
- Registered observation, likewise not fixed: the two recursive scans for
  module-access tokens that this deliverable is checked against cannot return
  zero across the whole documentation tree, because the register they refer to
  carries such a token inside its own required filename, and three migration
  documents plus the README and the setup page link to it by that name.  Those
  files are outside this file's scope and were not modified.  Within
  docs/migration/smoke-evidence the count is zero, and this file keeps it zero.
- expected-evidence, and every line so labelled is an EXPECTATION derived by
  reading the repository at commit c8f6226105, never an observation of a run.
  Nothing below was witnessed on this host, and every anchor below should be
  re-read at that commit rather than at the current tip.
- expected-evidence: the archive deploys under a fixed name.  README.md:139 at
  the base commit records the build output as
  acm-standard-applications/arkcase/target/arkcase-(version).war, and
  README.md:141 records that it is copied to $TOMCAT_HOME, renamed to
  arkcase.war, moved to $TOMCAT_HOME/webapps, and that the log to watch is
  $TOMCAT_HOME/logs/catalina.out.  The context name is therefore expected in the
  captured region and is preserved rather than normalised.
- expected-evidence: the first-startup window is 5 to 10 minutes, documented in
  two independent places - README.md:141 states it will take 5 - 10 minutes, and
  docs/setup.md:108 repeats that the first startup takes 5 to 10 minutes.  A
  region shorter than that window is presumptively truncated.
- expected-evidence: the container is Tomcat 9, per README.md:29, configured with
  the Apache Portable Runtime lifecycle listener carrying SSLEngine="on" and
  useAprConnector="true", per README.md:105-109.  Container identity and
  connector setup are therefore expected near the head of the region, with port
  assignments normalised away and component and class names preserved.
- expected-evidence: the region ends at the point the application answers at
  https://arkcase-ce.local/arkcase, per README.md:147.  The failure criterion a
  reader should apply is recorded at README.md:143 - a 404 from that URL after
  Tomcat has started means startup failed - and docs/setup.md:108 states the same
  criterion.  A container that starts is not the same thing as an application
  that started.
- expected-evidence: ArkCase serves a self-signed TLS certificate, per
  README.md:45.  This is why the sibling script's insecure-transport option is a
  correctness requirement rather than laziness when no certificate authority
  bundle is supplied: without it every probe fails certificate verification
  before reaching the application, and the run yields a false negative that would
  be indistinguishable from a startup failure.
- expected-evidence: warning and error lines are expected in a healthy first
  startup of this application and are captured as observed, with their message
  text preserved in full.  The dedicated error-severity scan is a sibling file;
  this region is the window that scan reads from, which is why the two must be
  drawn from the same capture and why this region is the foundational one.
- Mirror obligation.  The counterpart is migrated/startup/catalina.out.log: the
  same filename, the same field set in the same order, and the same
  region-selection rule stated above, so that a recursive diff between the two
  capture directories is mechanically meaningful.  A field added on one side
  only, or a region selected differently on one side only, would register as a
  difference that carries no behavioural signal at all.
